WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.130
Avengers: Endgame brought plenty of changes
to the Earth's Mightiest Heroes, and one of

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.080
the biggest was to Thor, who's put on a lot
of weight.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.089
"Fat Thor," as he's being called, is dealing
with a lot of depression and trauma.

00:00:13.089 --> 00:00:14.089
Here's his story.

00:00:14.089 --> 00:00:15.089
Traumavengers

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:19.070
From Black Panther's anger after his father's
murder to Iron Man's post-Avengers panic attacks

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.800
to Valkyrie abandoning Asgard and drowning
her sorrows, the Marvel movies have often

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.780
dealt with the psychological trauma that comes
with the life of a superhero.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.620
This time, though, it's on a completely different
scale.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:35.059
In the MCU, the ending of Infinity War is
unquestionably the biggest tragedy in the

00:00:35.059 --> 00:00:36.550
history of the planet.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:41.280
It's not just the billions of people turned
to dust, but as we see in Endgame, it's all

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.980
life, meaning that Thanos took out animals,
too.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.809
It's devastating for the entire universe,
and the heroes who were at the center of it

00:00:47.809 --> 00:00:49.309
are no exception.

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:53.750
When the film shifts its focus to five years
after the snap, we see that each of the Avengers

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.409
has been dealing with the trauma in a different
way.

00:00:56.409 --> 00:00:59.489
Even before we get to Thor, it's not always
healthy.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:04.100
In what's probably the most productive response,
we see Captain America leading a support group

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.690
for survivors, which works on a lot of levels.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:10.940
It highlights the empathy and moral center
that makes Steve Rogers such an appealing

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.860
character, and works as a tribute to his fallen
friend, Sam Wilson.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.160
Before he was an Avenger, Sam was leading
a support group for veterans, and with him

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.190
gone, Cap is doing his best to carry on that
legacy.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:26.090
The others, however, aren't doing so well
in the way they deal with tragedy.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:31.050
Hawkeye goes to an incredibly violent extreme,
trying to personally enforce a fairness that's

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.150
absent in the loss of innocent life.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:38.070
Black Widow loses herself in obsessing over
her work, trying to fix everything she can.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:42.500
While Tony Stark's life post-snap is far more
idyllic than the other heroes', he's still

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:47.050
completely given up making the world a better,
safer place, which defined his life as Iron

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.050
Man.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:52.520
He's refusing to use his genius to help others
because, well, what's the point?

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.310
He tried, it didn't work, time to move on.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:59.030
These are all very different and very relatable
ways of dealing with a traumatic experience.

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.090
The contextual Hulk

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.400
Bruce Banner's reaction to the snap is worth
paying close attention to, both because it

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.160
represents the biggest change for one of the
characters, and because of the relationship

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.420
he's had with Thor throughout the history
of the MCU.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.760
Since they first slugged it out on the helicarrier
back in the first Avengers film, they've been

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.270
competitors, and the buddy comedy of Thor:
Ragnarok put them in direct contrast with

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:21.270
each other.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.870
It's come up several times throughout the
films that since they hadn't met Captain Marvel

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.490
yet, they both consider themselves to be the
"strongest Avenger."

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.690
"Welcome, Strongest Avenger."

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:31.850
"Uh, what?"

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:36.260
For all their similarities, it's how they
react to their failures that shows how different

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.730
they really are.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:41.950
Since his character is entirely built around
making explosive anger and the reaction to

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.630
trauma a literal, physical change, it makes
sense that it's the Hulk that we first see

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:49.790
reacting to the devastation that comes along
with Thanos.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:54.690
This unstoppable force of nature comes up
against something he can't beat through strength.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:58.910
In Infinity War, Hulk learns the same thing
Thor learns at the beginning of Endgame.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:03.069
Sometimes, physical strength is completely
meaningless, and that's a devastating lesson

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:06.930
for someone who relies on that strength as
one of their defining characteristics.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.390
For the Hulk, the initial reaction is another
one of those unhealthy coping tactics.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.170
He runs away and hides.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.990
He won't even Hulk out when his life's in
danger, leaving Bruce Banner free of his dark

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.220
side for the first time in years.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.960
Instead of accepting this thing he's wanted,
Banner comes to understand that the Hulk is

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.340
a part of him, and as a result, he spends
the five-year gap essentially going through

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:32.120
the gamma-powered superheroic version of therapy
and working through his issues to unify his

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.120
personalities.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:42.560
"18 months in the gamma lab … now look at
me: Best of both worlds."

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.489
Back in the first Avengers film, Bruce Banner
gave us the iconic line that summed up his

00:03:46.489 --> 00:03:47.960
struggle with his emotions.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.120
I'm always angry."

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.709
The Hulk we have in Endgame, however, is no
longer driven by anger.

00:03:56.709 --> 00:04:00.569
He has young fans that want to take pictures
with him, and he shares his lunch with Ant-Man

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:03.129
in a scene that's far more than just a punchline.

00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:07.750
This is a brand-new version of the Hulk, one
who's not just smart, but kind and generous

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:12.230
instead of being a mindlessly angry walking
temper tantrum with the potential to destroy

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:13.230
a city.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:17.239
For the first time in the franchise, Bruce
Banner is happy because he's learned and worked

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:18.830
to change himself.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:21.630
Unfortunately, that's one thing Thor can't
do.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:22.630
Twilight of the gods

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:26.280
In a lot of modern stories about mythological
characters, including recent comics about

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.870
Thor, the idea is that gods are the embodiments
of ideas.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.669
Unlike humans, they can't change their nature.

00:04:32.669 --> 00:04:41.520
"Loki, I thought the world of you … at the
end of the day, you're you and I'm me."

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.010
Thor is the God of Thunder, who storms in
with a bolt of lightning and a booming hammer,

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.900
laying waste to his enemies.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.800
He's an almost effortlessly powerful warrior,
and the entire arc of Ragnarok is devoted

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.980
to the way he learns that his strength and
power come from within himself, not from any

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.510
of his magic weapons.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.900
It's his defining feature.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.070
So what happens when it doesn't work?

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:06.720
In Endgame, Thor sees himself as personally
responsible for failing to kill Thanos before

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.280
he could snap, and when he finally did finish
off the Mad Titan, it was too late to fix

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.280
anything.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.750
He goes through almost the same thing that
the Hulk does, except that he's made to watch

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:20.080
as the last remnants of his people are attacked,
and eventually finds out that even his shiny

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.780
new hammer can't stop Thanos in time.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.240
For all his strength, he failed.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.450
If his strength defines him, but it couldn't
save the billions of lives lost in Infinity

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.510
War, what's the point?

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:34.360
That's the existential crisis Thor's dealing
with in Endgame.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:35.360
Depression and substance abuse

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.620
When we meet back up with Thor five years
after the snap, he's almost completely inert.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:44.040
That's a very common and very relatable symptom
of depression, and so is the idea that he's

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.720
not just sitting around in the dark being
sad.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:50.669
For all the film's over-the-top visuals and
jokes about his weight, the movie isn't a

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:53.610
cartoonish portrayal of what it's like to
deal with depression.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:57.050
We see Thor laughing and hanging out with
his friends, but when he's confronted with

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:01.680
the idea that he could do something important,
the same thing he's failed at before, it just

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:02.990
doesn't matter.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:07.210
There's another element of his depression
that manifests itself in the film, too: he's

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:08.990
become an alcoholic.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:13.310
Thor's legendary appetite for beer has always
been a source of comedy in the MCU films.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:15.090
"I don't drink tea."

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:16.870
"What do you drink?"

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.470
"Not tea."

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.460
Endgame shows us the darker side of that.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:24.480
Like a lot of people with depression, including
Valkyrie in Ragnarok, Thor is self-medicating,

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.560
and his physical change is the result of that.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.570
Sadly, that's a pretty relatable element for
a lot of viewers, too.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:31.570
Voluminous and alone

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:35.960
One of the reasons Thor's "new body" was such
a surprise is that unlike a lot of Endgame's

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:39.380
other callbacks, there's no real precedent
for it in the comics.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:43.850
Thor has undergone plenty of changes over
the years, including the time he was a six-foot-six

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:48.490
frog for a few issues, but he's always been
drawn with the heroic proportions that you'd

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:49.490
expect.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:53.520
As far as his mental state, the closest we've
gotten to seeing a truly depressed Thor came

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.030
when we got a glimpse of a distant future
where Thor was the last surviving Asgardian.

00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:02.530
Like his movie counterpart, "Allfather Thor"
was dealing with isolation, depression, and

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:06.620
the toll of millennia of battle that had left
him with one arm and one eye.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:10.500
The main difference was that this Thor was
more than willing to take up his hammer and

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:13.580
fight, although his goal was a glorious death
in battle.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.520
That doesn't mean the Thor comics have been
free of fat jokes.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.970
For decades, one of the most prominent members
of Thor's supporting cast was Volstagg the

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:23.880
Voluminous, one of the Warriors Three.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:28.490
He's a once-mighty warrior whose waistline
is just as expanded as the inflated tales

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:29.490
of his glory.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:34.300
While Volstagg was often depicted as a braggart
and more than a little cowardly, he's also

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:38.080
always been a very positive character who
comes through in dire situations.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:43.150
Also, in an interesting contrast of what we
see with Thor, he once survived the destruction

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:48.130
of Asgard, only to waste away from lack of
food before Thor found him and nicknamed him

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:49.430
"Volstagg the Thin."

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:50.430
Relatable or insulting?

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:54.280
All of that forms the background, but for
some members of Endgame's audience, the possible

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.760
intent doesn't matter as much as what makes
it to the screen.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.509
The simple fact is that the reveal of "Fat
Thor" is absolutely played for laughs, and

00:08:02.509 --> 00:08:04.960
Thor's body is used as a frequent punchline.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.840
There are cracks from our heroes about Thor
having "Cheez Wiz running through his veins,"

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.949
and even the scene with his mother, one of
Endgame's most emotional sequences, ends with

00:08:12.949 --> 00:08:15.060
Frigga telling him to "eat a salad."

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.100
Part of that undoubtedly comes from the fact
that over the past decade, Thor has evolved

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:22.620
into a much more comedic character than he
might've been if Chris Hemsworth didn't turn

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.040
out to have a talent for comedy.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.460
"He transformed himself into a snake, and he knows that I love snakes.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:32.600
So, I went to pick up the snake to admire it and he transformed back into himself and he was like, 'Yeah, it’s me!' And he stabbed me."

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.940
That said, once he's done playing "Fat Thor,"
Hemsworth gets to go back to being, well,

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:38.940
Chris Hemsworth.

00:08:38.940 --> 00:08:43.270
The fat jokes are bound to sting regardless,
but coming from a dude known for being superheroically

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:47.100
ripped and ridiculously handsome, it definitely
feels like he's punching down.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.100
The reaction

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.860
For some viewers, the jokes at Thor's expense
were easy to forgive.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:54.600
Not only did they see Thor's depression and
its results as relatable, but the fact that

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.770
his fellow Avengers are lashing out is an
equally realistic depiction of how people

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.170
around them can react under stress.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:04.671
These characters have always been quick to
get snarky with each other, and that's part

00:09:04.671 --> 00:09:05.671
of their appeal.

00:09:05.671 --> 00:09:09.709
"Sometimes I want to punch you in your perfect
teeth."

00:09:09.709 --> 00:09:13.950
But even within the context of the film, we're
still seeing superheroes, the ultimate good

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:16.640
guys, making fun of someone's appearance.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:20.390
Context is important, but it's always worth
remembering that this is fiction, and calling

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:24.709
anything "realistic" doesn't hold much water
in a movie where a guy with rocket boots uses

00:09:24.709 --> 00:09:27.820
magic space rocks to fight a 7-foot purple
alien.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:31.890
For some critics, it just raises the question
of whether we would've lost anything if it

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:36.000
hadn't been there or if Thor's depression
had been shown in some other way.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.000
The defense of Thicc Thor

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.500
The flip side to the anger is that for all
its flaws and the sniping from other characters,

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.040
the action of the movie never really presents
Thor's body as a problem.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:48.800
The climax of that emotional scene with Frigga
is the present-day, post-snap Thor summoning

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:53.370
Mjolnir and realizing that despite the guilt
he's been feeling for five years, he's still

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:57.550
as worthy, heroic, and noble as he has always
been.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:01.610
Most telling, at the end of the movie, where
he truly regains his godly power and makes

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:07.160
a stand against Thanos, he doesn't just magically
transform back into his usual, muscular body.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:11.610
The only change to his physical appearance
is that his unkempt beard is now neatly braided.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:15.390
He's still as hefty as he was, but he's also
as strong as ever.

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:19.510
As corny might sound, all he really needed
was to talk to someone who understands what

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:20.640
he's going through.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:24.690
The fact that there's no physical difference
between the depressed, apathetic Thor of the

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:28.620
beginning of the film and the hero we see
at the end seems to indicate the filmmakers'

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:33.500
sincerity in presenting the depression, not
the weight gain, as the problem to be conquered.

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:37.230
Still, the jokes at his expense are very much
a part of the film, and it's hard to believe

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:41.670
that Thor won't be showing up in future MCU
films with his traditional jacked bodybuilder

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:42.670
physique.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:46.320
The way it's treated going forward is going
to go a long way in determining how audiences

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.040
look back on it here.

